22:18:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Colombia has a real history of drug-cartel violence and cocaine trafficking, and that history has shaped international perceptions. It is fair to acknowledge that. But it does **not** follow that “most guys there are wrapped up in it somehow.” Colombia has tens of millions of people working ordinary jobs, raising families, studying, and running legitimate businesses. Treating an individual neighbor as suspicious because of his nationality is an unfair leap—and it is also a poor way to assess business risk. For his business, the practical answer is the same as for any owner who may encounter customer skepticism: - operate legally and visibly; - provide licenses, insurance, clear contracts, receipts, and tax documentation where applicable; - use transparent pricing and refund/service policies; - build local references and authentic reviews; - communicate professionally and follow through reliably; - separate personal and business finances and use standard payment channels. Those steps give customers concrete reasons to trust him. Trust should be based on his conduct and the business’s records, not on Colombia’s past or media associations.
