22:19:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It makes sense that repeated news coverage can create that impression—but news and crime reporting are not a representative sample of everyday people. Drug-bust stories selectively focus on dramatic cases, and nationality is often highlighted when it fits an existing narrative. You are far less likely to see headlines about the enormous number of Colombians who go to work, operate businesses, or live ordinary lives precisely because those are not newsworthy events. That is a form of selection and availability bias: memorable examples become mentally “typical” even when they are statistically unrepresentative. Colombia has been involved in major international drug-trafficking networks historically, so Colombian nationals may appear in some reporting. But that cannot establish that a particular Colombian person—or “most guys” from Colombia—is involved. Criminal involvement has to be assessed from actual evidence: behavior, credentials, financial and business records, legal compliance, and references. For your neighbor’s business, judge it as you would any other: verify registration and licensing, read real customer reviews, use written agreements, and pay through traceable channels. Those are sensible precautions without making nationality a proxy for wrongdoing.
