22:19:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A country’s role in producing an illegal commodity does not mean most of its people—much less most of its men—participate in it. Drug trafficking is run by relatively small networks that exploit particular regions, corruption, coercion, and illicit supply chains. The people affected by it include far more victims than participants: farmers pressured into growing coca, communities exposed to violence, police, journalists, business owners, and families. Colombia’s population is around 50 million; it would be unreasonable to infer mass criminal involvement from a trafficking industry operated by a minority. Also, *Narcos* is dramatized entertainment based on real events, not a representative portrait of Colombian society. It concentrates on extraordinary cartel figures because that is the premise of the show—not on the ordinary lives of millions of Colombians. You can reasonably take precautions with **any** new business: check registration, licenses, insurance, references, contracts, and reviews. But there is no sound basis to treat your neighbor as suspect because he is Colombian. Judge the individual business’s evidence and conduct.
